[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Generics, Associated Types, and Type Erasure in iOS (Swift)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Swift provides powerful abstractions through Generics, Protocol Associated Types (ATRs), and Type Erasure. Mastering these concepts allows you to build highly reusable, type-safe, and modular iOS architectures."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Generics"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics allow you to write flexible, reusable functions and types that can work with any data type, subject to requirements that you define. They prevent code duplication and enforce compile-time safety."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"struct Stack<T> {
    private var elements: [T] = []

    mutating func push(_ element: T) {
        elements.append(element)
    }

    mutating func pop() -> T? {
        return elements.popLast()
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Protocols with Associated Types (PATs)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When defining protocols that need to work with unspecified types, you use Associated Types via the associatedtype keyword. This acts as a placeholder type until the protocol is adopted by a concrete implementation."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"protocol DataSource {
    associatedtype Item
    func numberOfItems() -> Int
    func item(at index: Int) -> Item
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. The Type Erasure Problem"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Protocols with associated types cannot be used as standalone types or stored inside heterogeneous collections. If you try to declare an array like [DataSource], Swift throws the famous compiler error: Protocol 'DataSource' can only be used as a generic constraint because it has Self or associated type requirements."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Implementing Type Erasure"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Type erasure solves this by wrapping the PAT in a concrete, non-generic wrapper class or struct (often prefixed with Any). The wrapper hides the underlying associated type while forwarding method calls."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"class AnyDataSource<T>: DataSource {
    private let _numberOfItems: () -> Int
    private let _item: (Int) -> T

    init<U: DataSource>(_ dataSource: U) where U.Item == T {
        self._numberOfItems = dataSource.numberOfItems
        self._item = dataSource.item(at:)
    }

    func numberOfItems() -> Int {
        return _numberOfItems()
    }

    func item(at index: Int) -> T {
        return _item(index)
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Takeaways"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Generics provide type-safe code reusability across data structures and functions."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Associated Types make protocols flexible by deferring concrete type definitions to conforming types."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Type Erasure bridges the limitation of PATs, allowing them to be stored in arrays or used as property types."}]
